s"},{"season":2021,"team":"NYG","distance":0.9465,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Patrick Graham"},{"season":2020,"team":"GB","distance":0.9619,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Chargers went 11-6 in 2025 and outscored opponents 368 to 340. The defense did most of the work, finishing 7th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 25th in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the 2nd highest 10 personnel usage and the fewest pass rushers in the league.","offense":"The Chargers offense was well below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 2nd highest 10 personnel usage and the fewest tight ends on the field in the league. Justin Herbert ran the passing game, with Keenan Allen leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Chargers had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 7th or better in three of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per rush, where it ranked 3rd, and it had no real weak spot. Tuli Tuipulotu was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Odafe Oweh, who had 7.5 sacks while playing only 49% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the fewest pass rushers in the league.","special":"The Chargers were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 93% of field goals, which was one of the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 20% of the money on the books.","players":"Justin Herbert played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,727 yards. Keenan Allen was the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 777 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Kimani Vidal and Omarion Hampton. On defense, Tuli Tuipulotu led the team with 13 sacks and Donte Jackson picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Greg Roman called the offense and Jesse Minter called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Cowboys under Mike McCarthy and the 2025 Texans under Nick Caley. Their defense looked most like the 2020 Rams under Brandon Staley and the 2019 Packers.","trends":"The offense went from 12th in EPA per play in 2024 to 25th in 2025, a big step back. The defense moved from 7th in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 7th in 2025, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was 10 personnel usage, which went from 0% to 5%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}